    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What happens in a couples session?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">Sessions are usually held with both of you and last 45 minutes. The psychologist holds the conversation and makes sure there is room for each of you to speak. At some points in the process, separate meetings with each of you may also take place.</p>
        <ol class="space-y-5">
          <li class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">1. First meeting</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We listen to both of you and to the history of the relationship. We ask each of you separately what you are hoping for from therapy — the answers are often not the same, and seeing that early is useful.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">2. Mapping the pattern</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We open up the repeating cycle together: what triggers what, who withdraws and when, where the argument always ends up. The subject is not who is right but how the cycle works.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">3. Trying it in the room</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Some of the difficult conversations happen inside the session. The point is for that conversation to become possible at home too. This is usually the hardest part of the process and the part that does the most.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">4. Reviewing where we are</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">At regular intervals we look together at where the process has got to. The decision to continue or to pause is made with both of you.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Does the psychologist take sides?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">No. What is worked on in couples therapy is not the two people but the pattern between them. No ruling is made about who is right — such a ruling would not change the relationship anyway.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">That does not mean everything is met in the middle. In some situations one person is carrying more, and that is named as what it is. The distinction is between taking sides and making what is there visible.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">A session in which one of you does not feel heard does not work. That is watched for.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What is the difference between couples, family and individual therapy?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">Each works with a different unit. Which one to start with usually becomes clear in the first meeting, and moving between them is possible.</p>
